<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniele Manin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniele Manin was a Venetian lawyer and patriot who led the Republic of San Marco in 1848–1849. Freed from Austrian imprisonment by crowds in March 1848, he proclaimed the republic and as its president organised the city's defence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held out under siege, bombardment and cholera until August 1849, after which Manin went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniele-manin.jpg" alt="Daniele Manin" width="274" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. Voland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniele_Manin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manin was born Daniele Fonseca in Ramo Astori, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, on 13 May 1804. His mother, Anna Maria Bellotto, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, and his father's family from Verona. His grandfather Samuele Medina was Jewish and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1759, taking the name Manin because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>, sponsored his conversion. Daniele studied law at the University of Padua and from an early age hated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which then ruled Venice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1847 he presented a petition to the Venetian congregation, a consultative assembly tolerated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, setting out the wants of the nation, and he was arrested for high treason on 18 January 1848. The crowds forced the Austrian governor, Count Pallfy, to release him on 17 March, revolutionaries seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-arsenal">Venetian Arsenal</a>, and the Austrians withdrew on 26 March.</p><p>Manin handed his powers to Piedmontese commissioners on 7 August 1848, but after King Charles Albert's defeat at Custoza and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> that abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, he saved the commissioners from a lynching crowd and headed a triumvirate. Chosen president again early in 1849, he directed the defence with the Neapolitan general Guglielmo Pepe. Fort Marghera was abandoned on 26 May, the powder magazine blew up on 19 June, and Austrian batteries bombarded the city. On 24 August 1849 Manin negotiated an amnesty under which he, Pepe and others went into exile, and he left on a French ship on 27 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His wife died in Marseille on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where Manin became a leader of the Italian exiles. Turning from republicanism to monarchy, he founded the Società Nazionale Italiana with Giorgio Pallavicini and Giuseppe La Farina to spread the idea of unity under King Victor Emmanuel. His daughter died in 1854. Manin died on 22 September 1857 and was buried in Ary Scheffer's family tomb. In 1868, two years after the Austrians left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, his remains were brought home for a public funeral and placed in a sarcophagus in the Piazzetta dei Leoncini beside the Basilica San Marco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daniele Manin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As revolution spread across Europe, crowds freed the lawyer <b>Daniele Manin</b> from prison on 17 March 1848, and on 22 March the Venetians rose against the Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia and took the Arsenal.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-san-marco">Republic of San Marco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in former 2nd arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Daniele Manin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
